WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:05.737
The courtroom was silent with tension as the presiding judge glared at the proud woman standing before him.

2
00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:09.457
She had been arrested and tried, and the verdict was final.

3
00:00:09.458 --> 00:00:14.241
She was found guilty. Her crime? Voting in an election.

4
00:00:14.242 --> 00:00:20.397
At the time of the trial, in 1873, it was against the law for women to vote in the United States.

5
00:00:20.398 --> 00:00:28.841
Women had no voice in how their government was run, no say in how laws were made, and no way to state their grievances against the government.

6
00:00:28.842 --> 00:00:34.881
Susan B. Anthony was born on February 15, 1820, on a farm in Massachusetts.

7
00:00:34.882 --> 00:00:45.209
She was raised in a Quaker family whose members were abolitionists—antislavery activists—and they profoundly affected Anthony’s sense of justice and equality from a young age.

8
00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:51.152
Other abolitionists met at her family’s farm almost every Sunday to fight for the freedom of black slaves.

9
00:00:51.153 --> 00:00:58.588
Among those who came to her home was Frederick Douglass, a runaway slave who became a prominent abolitionist and political leader.

10
00:00:58.589 --> 00:01:06.310
In 1846, at the age of 26, Anthony became head of the Female Department at Canajoharie Academy in New York.

11
00:01:06.311 --> 00:01:13.286
During her two years there, she experienced the inequality that all female teachers faced in the educational system at the time.

12
00:01:13.287 --> 00:01:19.285
Female teachers were not as respected as male teachers, and they were paid less than men for the same work.

13
00:01:19.286 --> 00:01:26.458
Even more unfair was that most young girls in America did not receive an adequate education; many received none at all.

14
00:01:26.459 --> 00:01:34.379
After her experience at Canajoharie, Susan B. Anthony was inspired to publicly argue for equal opportunities for men and women.

15
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.384
This concept was highly controversial at the time, as was advocating it in public.

16
00:01:39.385 --> 00:01:46.154
Anthony felt that all persons, regardless of their gender, should be allowed to attend any school, college, or university.

17
00:01:46.155 --> 00:01:56.149
Anthony believed that there was no difference between the male mind and the female mind, so she also advocated for coeducation and for men and women to be taught the same academic subjects.

18
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.633
However, her battle was not easy.

19
00:01:58.634 --> 00:02:01.186
Many male educators laughed at her.

20
00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:10.696
But to foreshadow what was to come in education, in 1855 the University of Iowa became the first public university to admit both genders on an equal basis.

21
00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:19.031
During the initial stage of her campaign for equal education, Anthony quickly became aware of how a woman’s role in society was not unlike that of a slave.

22
00:02:19.032 --> 00:02:22.571
Inequality in education was just one part of the problem.

23
00:02:22.572 --> 00:02:29.898
From then on, Susan B. Anthony devoted her life to fighting for women’s rights and found other men and women who felt the same way.

24
00:02:29.899 --> 00:02:38.722
In 1851, while in Seneca, New York, Anthony was introduced to Elizabeth Cady Stanton, one of the leaders of the new women’s rights movement.

25
00:02:38.723 --> 00:02:42.793
They quickly developed a strong partnership that would last many years.

26
00:02:42.794 --> 00:02:50.769
Anthony began wearing bloomers, baggy pants that were gathered at the ankles, a style of dress that was considered unladylike by many men and women.

27
00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:59.868
But wearing bloomers was a way for Susan B. Anthony to protest the status quo in society, and many early feminists loved to show off their new views through fashion.

28
00:02:59.869 --> 00:03:05.486
In 1852, Anthony attended her first woman’s rights convention in Syracuse, New York.

29
00:03:05.487 --> 00:03:13.691
After that, she and Stanton tirelessly campaigned door to door, in legislatures, and in many town meetings for the cause of women’s rights.

30
00:03:13.692 --> 00:03:24.457
However, Susan B. Anthony began to realize a fundamental truth: for women to make a difference in public affairs and in their homes, they needed women’s suffrage—the power to vote.

31
00:03:24.458 --> 00:03:34.468
In 1866, Anthony and Stanton founded the American Equal Rights Association to try to win voting rights for everyone, regardless of color or sex. In

32
00:03:34.469 --> 00:03:44.335
addition, two years later, the two women founded The Revolution , a newspaper whose aim was to secure the right to vote for women and African-Americans.

33
00:03:44.336 --> 00:03:52.510
The paper also discussed issues such as equal pay for equal work, divorce laws, and the church’s position on women’s rights.

34
00:03:52.511 --> 00:04:01.329
Soon, bloomer-wearing Anthony was recognized as the most famous advocate of the movement, and as a result, she became a target of public abuse.

35
00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:07.272
Elizabeth Stanton’s public image was more feminine and demure; she was a wife and mother.

36
00:04:07.273 --> 00:04:12.643
In contrast, Anthony remained unmarried and was portrayed as a cold, hard person.

37
00:04:12.644 --> 00:04:22.692
But Anthony did not allow public opinion to sway her; she tirelessly traveled and spoke at numerous halls and town meetings, never losing her focus on what she was fighting for.

38
00:04:22.693 --> 00:04:33.078
In 1868, the United States passed the Fourteenth Amendment, which stated that all people born in America were citizens and no legal privileges could be denied to any citizen.

39
00:04:33.079 --> 00:04:41.402
But section two of the amendment, by specifying African-American males as citizens, seemed to cast doubt on whether women were actually citizens.

40
00:04:41.403 --> 00:04:48.032
Susan B. Anthony decided to challenge this amendment and urged other women to do the same by voting in the next election.

41
00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:52.833
Women in ten states went to vote, but many were turned away at the polls.

42
00:04:52.834 --> 00:04:59.225
But on November 5, 1872, Anthony voted in a presidential election in Rochester, New York.

43
00:04:59.226 --> 00:05:04.988
At the voting booth, she claimed she had the right to vote under the U.S. Constitution and the Fourteenth Amendment.

44
00:05:04.989 --> 00:05:09.202
No one tried to stop her, but four days later, she was arrested.

45
00:05:09.203 --> 00:05:11.765
Susan B. Anthony was found guilty.

46
00:05:11.766 --> 00:05:15.539
Before sentencing her, the judge asked if she had anything to say.

47
00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:19.380
Anthony courageously stated she had a great many things to say.

48
00:05:19.381 --> 00:05:29.195
Standing up, she said in a calm voice: "My natural rights, my civil rights, my political rights, my judicial rights, are all alike ignored.

49
00:05:29.196 --> 00:05:45.292
Robbed of the fundamental privilege of citizenship, I am degraded from the status of a citizen to that of a subject; and not only myself individually, but all of my sex, are, by your honor’s verdict, doomed to political subjection under this, so-called, form of government."

50
00:05:45.293 --> 00:05:51.228
The judge tried to silence her and ordered her to pay a $100 fine. Anthony refused to pay it.

51
00:05:51.229 --> 00:05:55.676
After her trial, Susan B. Anthony was impelled to fight harder for her rights.

52
00:05:55.677 --> 00:06:03.717
In 1877, she gathered petitions from 26 states with 10,000 signatures of people demanding women’s suffrage.

53
00:06:03.718 --> 00:06:08.677
Anthony took those petitions to the U.S. Congress, only to be laughed at by the lawmakers.

54
00:06:08.678 --> 00:06:10.995
Still, she did not give up.

55
00:06:10.996 --> 00:06:18.435
She continued to appear before every session of Congress until 1906, the year of her death, to petition for a woman’s right to vote.

56
00:06:18.436 --> 00:06:24.699
By then, Susan B. Anthony’s public image had changed drastically. To many, she had become a heroine.

57
00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:31.326
Despite her undivided zeal for the cause, Susan B. Anthony did not see women’s suffrage realized during her lifetime.

58
00:06:31.327 --> 00:06:38.049
She died on March 13, 1906, and her last public words were, "Failure is impossible!"

59
00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:44.803
This became the motto for future advocates of women’s rights, who would carry on the campaign for justice and equality.

60
00:06:44.804 --> 00:06:52.120
In August 1920, the United States ratified the Nineteenth Amendment to the U.S. Constitution, which allowed women to vote.

61
00:06:52.121 --> 00:06:58.786
This amendment is also known as the Susan B. Anthony Amendment to honor the woman who dedicated her life to women’s rights.

62
00:06:58.787 --> 00:07:02.680
Anthony was the voice that spoke when others were afraid or indifferent.

63
00:07:02.681 --> 00:07:10.703
She lived and died for what she strongly believed in, and more importantly, Susan B. Anthony proved that women have the right to make a difference.

